<%# THE CONFIRM WAS REFUSED. Local: `errors` (an Array of strings, each naming the envelope
    whose line failed).

    Above everything else on the page, because the figures below it are a proposal again — the
    same one, unwritten — and a reader who scrolls past this banner would have no way to tell
    that from a screen they had just successfully confirmed.

    The second sentence is not reassurance, it is the fact that makes the first one safe:
    AllocationCommitter writes every line inside one transaction, so a refused line takes the
    whole split back out with it. An error message over a half-written ledger is the worst
    outcome available on this screen, and saying so is how the user knows they can simply fix
    the row and press the button again. %>
<div id="distribution-errors" class="rounded border border-status-danger bg-status-danger-light px-6 py-4">
  <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-status-danger">This split wasn't written</p>
  <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-700">
    Nothing moved — the ledger is exactly as it was. Fix the line below and confirm again.
  </p>
  <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-0.5 pl-5 text-sm text-status-danger">
    <% errors.each do |error| %>
      <li><%= error %></li>
    <% end %>
  </ul>
</div>
